network-guide.md:954: - The request carries no `cache-control: no-cache` / `no-store`, and no `authorization` or `cookie` header the caller attached itself.
network-guide.md:955: - The request's query has no key that names a secret, by the same rule `ApiLoggingInterceptor` redacts with (`ApiLoggingInterceptor.isSensitiveKey`: `token`, `otp`, `signature`, `sig`, `auth`, `api_key` and the rest; see the redaction table). A verification or reset link, a one-time code or a presigned URL is about that secret, and the store is plaintext, so such a request neither reads from nor writes to the cache - **when its secret travels under one of those key names**. The rule judges key names only; a presigned URL whose signature parameter has another name is not recognised, and its key belongs in the list. A query that does not parse is treated the same way. Only query **keys** are judged: a secret in a path segment (`/reset/<token>`) is not recognised, as it is not by the log redaction, so do not put one there.
network-guide.md:956: - The **response** carries no `Cache-Control` `no-store`, `private` or `no-cache` directive, no `Pragma: no-cache` and no `Set-Cookie`. The first two mark a body the server does not want in a shared or persistent cache - an anonymous email-verification or password-reset check, a presigned download. `no-cache` allows storage only with revalidation on every reuse, which this cache does not do. Such a response is passed through without being written, and any entry already stored for that URL is removed. **On Flutter web the `Set-Cookie` rule never fires:** browsers hide `Set-Cookie` from script (a forbidden response header in the Fetch spec), and dio's web adapter only sees what `getAllResponseHeaders()` returns. A web build relies on the server sending `Cache-Control: private` or `no-store` for a cookie-setting response.
network-guide.md:961: The credential check reads the token store rather than sniffing the outgoing `Authorization` header. dio runs `onRequest` in registration order and `CacheInterceptor` is registered before `AuthInterceptor`, so on the request leg that header does not exist yet - a header sniff there is dead code. Asking the store makes the decision correct wherever the interceptor sits in the chain.
network-guide.md:969: The cache also records, under `cache_rule_version`, the version of the sensitive-key rule (`ApiLoggingInterceptor.sensitiveKeyRuleVersion`) its entries were written under. When a build changes the rule - a new secret name makes some URLs secret queries, which the cache then never reads or evicts - the first request drops every cached entry, since a hashed key cannot say which URL it was. Entries with no recorded version (written by a build before this record) are dropped the same way. A purge that fails is retried on the next request. `clearCache()` removes the record with the entries. Bump `sensitiveKeyRuleVersion` with every change to the key sets; `cache_rule_version_test.dart` fails until you do.
